steps.text:
Yes — this is a bug in that revision’s `@apply` implementation.

`border-dotted` is emitted twice:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; } /* reset */
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

`@apply border-dotted` copies declarations from both matching rules, so the resulting rule includes `border-width: 0`, which suppresses the border. In regular markup, it can appear to work when combined with a width utility such as `border`, because the generated stylesheet’s source order lets that width declaration win.

The practical workaround is to apply the width and set the style explicitly:

```css
.my-rule {
  @apply border;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

or add a nonzero `border-width` after the `@apply`.

The underlying issue is that the “reset” was emitted under the same selector as the style utility, and `@apply` blindly merges all matching selector rules.
